kosha instruments

  -- the physical tools of engineering drawing (manual drafting).
  -- before CAD (computer-aided design), all engineering drawings were made by hand.
  -- mastery of instruments IS mastery of drawing — the hand trains the eye.
  --
  -- DRAWING BOARD AND PAPER:
  --   drawing board: flat, smooth surface. often with parallel motion bar.
  --   drafting machine: combines parallel motion + protractor head. replaces T-square + set squares.
  --   paper sizes: A0 (841×1189mm), A1, A2, A3, A4 (210×297mm). each half the previous.
  --   tracing paper: semi-transparent. for overlays and copying.
  --   polyester film (Mylar): dimensionally stable. archival quality.
  --
  -- DRAWING INSTRUMENTS:
  --   T-square:        horizontal lines parallel to drawing board edge.
  --   set squares:     45°/45°/90° and 30°/60°/90° triangles. combined for various angles.
  --   protractor:      measuring and marking angles. 180° or 360°.
  --   compass:         drawing arcs and circles. set to radius, pivot on centre point.
  --   dividers:        transferring distances. two needle points, no pencil.
  --   French curves:   smooth irregular curves. set of plastic templates.
  --   flexicurve:      adjustable flexible ruler for smooth curves.
  --   circle template: pre-cut circles for small diameters faster than compass.
  --   ruling pen:      ink lines of controlled width. used with set squares.
  --
  -- PENCILS AND GRADES:
  --   hardness scale: 9H (hardest) → H, F, HB, B → 9B (softest).
  --   9H-4H: very hard. light lines. construction lines. not for finished drawing.
  --   2H-H:  hard. thin visible lines. outlines.
  --   HB-F:  medium. lettering. dimension lines.
  --   B-2B:  soft. freehand sketching.
  --   mechanical pencil: 0.3, 0.5, 0.7mm lead. consistent width without sharpening.
  --
  -- LINE TYPES (what each line means):
  --   visible outline:       thick continuous.  the edges you can see.
  --   hidden detail:         thin dashed.       edges hidden behind visible surfaces.
  --   centre line:           thin chain (−·−·). centres of circles, axes of symmetry.
  --   cutting plane:         thick chain + arrows. where section cut is taken.
  --   dimension/extension:   thin continuous.   measurement annotation.
  --   break line:            thin continuous wavy or zigzag. material removed for space.
  --   hatching:              thin continuous 45°. cut surfaces in section views.
  --
  -- CAD (Computer-Aided Design):
  --   replaces all manual instruments with software.
  --   2D CAD: AutoCAD — same drawing conventions, mouse instead of pencil.
  --   3D CAD: SolidWorks, CATIA, Fusion 360 — model first, drawing derived.
  --   parametric: dimensions drive the geometry (change dimension → shape updates).
  --   BIM (Building Information Modelling): 3D model contains all drawing info + cost + schedule.

